<article class="article">
  <div class="eyebrow">nanogpt-served · llm inference</div>
  <h1 class="post-title">PagedAttention from scratch</h1>
  <p class="post-sub">Three pieces let a KV cache survive a churning batch: a block
  allocator, a gather kernel, and a preemption policy. Built by hand and measured —
  naive reservation wastes <b>84.6%</b> of the pool, paging brings it to <b>2.8%</b>,
  and my preemption policy won one workload and lost the other.</p>
  <div class="post-meta">Balaji Chidambaram · August 2026 · ~9 min read</div>

<p>The <a href="continuous-batching-from-scratch.html">last post</a> ended on an
admission. I'd built an iteration-level scheduler that re-decided the batch every
decode step, and then had it recompute the entire context on every step, because a KV
cache didn't fit. The closing section was called
<em>"why a KV cache doesn't just drop in."</em> This is the answer.</p>

<p>Everything below is in
<a href="https://github.com/balachidam21/nanogpt-served">nanogpt-served</a>, and every
number comes from running it.</p>

<h2><span class="n">01</span>The cache that didn't fit</h2>

<p>A KV cache is simple when you serve one request: as each token is generated you
append its key and value vectors to a growing tensor, so the next step attends over
history instead of recomputing it. One sequence, one length, one contiguous block of
memory.</p>

<p>Continuous batching breaks all three assumptions at once. Membership changes every
step. Lengths differ per request and grow at different rates. A request can finish at
step 3 and free its memory in the middle of the pool. There is no single contiguous
tensor to append to, which is why my scheduler cheated and recomputed.</p>

<p>That's the smaller problem. The larger one appears the moment you try to fix it the
obvious way. You don't know how long a request will run — generation stops on an
end-of-sequence token, not a schedule — so the safe move is to reserve the maximum:
<code class="inl">max_seq_len</code> worth of KV for every admitted request, up front.</p>

<p>Here is what that costs. Three requests that end up using 37, 900, and 12 tokens,
with <code class="inl">max_seq_len = 2048</code>:</p>

