<div class="keypt">
  <b>Why the waste matters.</b> Decode is memory-bound: at batch size 1 the GPU spends
  nearly all of a step moving weights out of HBM and almost none of it doing arithmetic.
  Batching fixes that, because one weight-read serves many requests. But how many you can
  batch is capped by how many KV caches fit in memory. Waste 84.6% of that memory and
  you've capped your own throughput at a sixth of what the hardware could do.
</div>

<h2><span class="n">02</span>The idea is fifty years old</h2>

<p>PagedAttention doesn't invent anything. It recognizes that KV-cache fragmentation is
the same problem operating systems solved for RAM in the 1960s, and reuses the solution
directly.</p>

<p>A process wants a big contiguous address space. Physical RAM is fragmented. The OS
chops memory into fixed-size <b>pages</b>, gives each process a <b>page table</b> mapping
its virtual pages to whatever physical frames are free, and puts a piece of hardware —
the <b>MMU</b> — in the path of every load and store to walk that table. The process gets
its illusion of contiguity; physical memory stays a free-for-all.</p>

<p>Swap four nouns and you have PagedAttention:</p>

<table class="small">
  <thead><tr><th>Operating system</th><th>PagedAttention</th><th>In my code</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>page (fixed-size)</td><td>block — 16 token-slots</td><td><code class="inl">BlockPool</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>page table (per process)</td><td>block table (per request)</td><td><code class="inl">BlockTable.block_ids</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>page-table walk</td><td>logical position → physical slot</td><td><code class="inl">translate()</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>MMU (does the fetch)</td><td>the gather kernel</td><td><code class="inl">gather_kv()</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="keypt">
  Attention needs to read every past key and value. It has no opinion about whether they
  sit next to each other. Contiguity was an artifact of how the allocator handed out
  memory, and one level of indirection removes it.
</div>

<h2><span class="n">03</span>Part 1 — the allocator</h2>

<p>Two objects. A <b>pool</b> owns physical blocks and knows nothing about requests. A
<b>block table</b> belongs to one request and knows nothing about other requests. Neither
has any idea what a tensor is — the file imports no torch at all, because paging is
integer bookkeeping. vLLM draws the same line, which is why its block manager knows
nothing about CUDA.</p>

