<p>Address translation is two integer operations against a list lookup. vLLM uses block
size 16 — a power of two — so in the hot path both become a shift and a mask. No
division.</p>

<p>The one real decision in the allocator is <b>when</b> to grab a new block:</p>

<pre><code><span class="c"># LAZY: allocate only when a token has nowhere to go.</span>
<span class="k">def</span> append_token(self):
    <span class="c"># True when the tail block is full — AND when there are no</span>
    <span class="c"># blocks at all. One modulo covers both cases, no special-casing.</span>
    <span class="k">if</span> self.num_tokens % self.pool.block_size == 0:
        blk = self.pool.allocate()
        <span class="k">if</span> blk <span class="k">is</span> None:
            <span class="k">return</span> False          <span class="c"># pool dry — the scheduler's problem now</span>
        self.block_ids.append(blk)
    self.num_tokens += 1
    <span class="k">return</span> True</code></pre>

<p>Lazy allocation caps waste at <code class="inl">block_size − 1</code> per request.
Eager — grabbing the next block the instant the tail fills — caps it at
<code class="inl">block_size</code>, because a request that stops <em>exactly</em> on a
block boundary is left holding an empty block it never writes to. One slot of difference,
and it only appears in the aligned case.</p>

<p>Two smaller things. <code class="inl">allocate()</code> returns
<code class="inl">None</code> instead of raising, because running out of blocks is a
normal operating condition — under load the pool <em>will</em> go dry, and that's a signal
to the scheduler, not a crash. And the waste bound is structural: every interior block is
full by construction, so all of a request's slack lives in its tail block.</p>

<p>The bound is easy to state and easy to get wrong, so I measured it:</p>

<table>
