<p>The obvious implementation is the page-table walk drawn T times:</p>

<pre><code><span class="k">def</span> gather_kv_loop(pool_tensor, table):
    rows = []
    <span class="k">for</span> pos <span class="k">in</span> range(table.num_tokens):
        block, offset = table.translate(pos)     <span class="c"># the page-table walk</span>
        rows.append(pool_tensor[block, offset])  <span class="c"># (d,) one token's vector</span>
    <span class="k">return</span> torch.stack(rows)                    <span class="c"># (T, d)</span></code></pre>

<p>The vectorized version computes all T addresses at once. The trick is to stop
thinking in <code class="inl">(block, offset)</code> pairs and give every token a single
integer address — flatten the pool from
<code class="inl">(num_blocks, block_size, d)</code> to
<code class="inl">(num_blocks · block_size, d)</code>, so
<code class="inl">slot = physical_block · block_size + offset</code>. Once every token
has one address, "gather" is one index:</p>

<pre><code><span class="k">def</span> gather_kv(pool_tensor, table):
    num_blocks, block_size, d = pool_tensor.shape
    T = table.num_tokens

    blocks  = torch.tensor(table.block_ids)      <span class="c"># (L,)  logical order</span>
    base    = blocks * block_size                <span class="c"># (L,)  first slot of each block</span>
    offsets = torch.arange(block_size)           <span class="c"># (block_size,)</span>
    slots   = base[:, None] + offsets            <span class="c"># (L, block_size) via broadcast</span>
    slots   = slots.reshape(-1)[:T]              <span class="c"># (T,)  flatten, drop the tail slack</span>

    flat = pool_tensor.view(num_blocks * block_size, d)
    <span class="k">return</span> flat[slots]                          <span class="c"># (T, d) — one indexing op</span></code></pre>

<p>Two details here matter. <code class="inl">.view</code> raises if the bytes can't be
reinterpreted for free; <code class="inl">.reshape</code> silently copies. On a
multi-gigabyte KV pool read every decode step, I want the one that errors.</p>

<p>And that blind <code class="inl">[:T]</code> slice — dropping the unfilled slots of
the last block — is only correct because of the invariant from §03. Decode only ever
appends, so every interior block is full and all the slack lives in the tail. If decode
could write into the middle of history, that line would return garbage silently.</p>

<p>Both versions produce bit-identical output — I checked with
<code class="inl">torch.equal</code>, not <code class="inl">allclose</code>, against a
naive contiguous baseline, with the request's blocks deliberately non-consecutive
(<code class="inl">[0, 2, 3]</code>). <code class="inl">max abs diff: 0.00e+00</code>.
Then I timed them at realistic size:</p>

