<p>The 29× itself isn't a PagedAttention result — it's ordinary Python overhead, and
anyone who has vectorized a loop before will have guessed the direction. What I hadn't
internalized is that a memory-traffic model predicts these two functions cost the
<em>same</em>, since both copy exactly <code class="inl">T·d</code> numbers. The cost
isn't in the copy. It's in the 2,048 separate trips through the interpreter to schedule
the copy.</p>

<p>Two things about this implementation would not survive contact with a real GPU.
<code class="inl">torch.tensor(table.block_ids)</code> rebuilds a tensor from a Python
list on every call, with no device placement — on CUDA that's a host-to-device transfer
per decode step, which reintroduces exactly the per-step overhead the vectorized version
was meant to remove. A real scheduler keeps block tables resident on device and updates
them in place.</p>

<div class="next">
  <b>The bigger problem: the copy shouldn't exist.</b> Materializing a contiguous
  <code class="inl">(T, d)</code> buffer means writing it into HBM and reading it straight
  back. At <code class="inl">T=2048</code>, 32 layers, 64 requests, fp16, that's
  <b>2.1 GB gathered per decode step</b> — and since a copy crosses the bus twice,
  <b>~4.3 GB of HBM traffic, roughly 1.4 ms at 3 TB/s</b>, spent moving data that was
  already in HBM. vLLM <b>fuses</b> the gather into the attention kernel so the contiguous
  copy is never formed. That, not the 29×, is why PagedAttention needed a CUDA kernel and
  not just a smarter indexing expression.
</div>

<h2><span class="n">05</span>Part 3 — what happens when the pool runs dry</h2>

<p>Paging bounds the waste; it doesn't create memory. Oversubscribe hard enough and
<code class="inl">allocate()</code> returns <code class="inl">None</code> while a running
request needs one more block to take its next step.</p>

<p>My instinct was to reach for the word <em>eviction</em>, and that instinct is wrong.
When a CPU cache evicts a line it discards a cold copy — RAM still has the data, nothing
is undone, nobody is harmed. A KV pool has no cold blocks and no backing store. Every
block belongs to a request that needs it on the next step.</p>

<div class="keypt">
  You can't free memory here without hurting someone; you can only choose whom. That's
  preemption, and it's a policy question, not an implementation detail.
</div>

<p>The cost of the choice has a clean formula. Preempting a request means releasing its
blocks and putting it back in the queue. Its generated <em>tokens</em> are integers and
are never lost — only the KV is. So:</p>

<pre><code>cost(preempt R) = R.prompt_len + R.generated    <span class="c"># tokens to recompute</span></code></pre>

<p>A resume is a prefill, only longer — so the price of preempting a request rises every
step it runs. All of this is CPU-side integer bookkeeping; the scheduler never touches
the GPU. It decides who is in the batch and who loses their blocks, while the GPU holds
the tensors and runs the kernels. That split is why
<code class="inl">paged_kv.py</code> imports no torch and
<code class="inl">paged_attention.py</code> does.</p>

<p>vLLM's real allocator has three tiers, and the one I built is the last of them:</p>

<div class="panel">
  <div class="scroll">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 620 200" role="img" aria-label="Three-tier block allocation: free list, evict cached, preempt running">
    <defs>
      <marker id="a3" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6.5" refY="3" orient="auto">
        <path d="M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 Z" fill="var(--muted)"/>
      </marker>
    </defs>
    <rect x="10" y="14" width="600" height="44" rx="8" fill="#eef7f0" stroke="#a7c9b0"/>
    <text x="26" y="34" class="s-lbl" fill="#3d7a52">1 · free list</text>
    <text x="26" y="50" class="s-sub">blocks nobody holds — take one, nobody is harmed</text>

    <rect x="10" y="72" width="600" height="44" rx="8" fill="#fdf6e6" stroke="#e8cf94"/>
    <text x="26" y="92" class="s-lbl" fill="#9a7208">2 · evict unreferenced cached blocks (LRU)</text>
    <text x="26" y="108" class="s-sub">real cold data — prefix-cache entries no live request needs</text>

    <rect x="10" y="130" width="600" height="52" rx="8" fill="#fbeeea" stroke="#dcae9c"/>
    <text x="26" y="150" class="s-lbl" fill="var(--accent2)">3 · preempt a running request</text>
    <text x="26" y="166" class="s-sub">no cold blocks left — someone loses work. THIS is where my policy runs.</text>
    <text x="26" y="180" class="s-sub" fill="var(--accent2)">select_victim() → release() → requeue → resume as a longer prefill</text>

    <line x1="315" y1="58" x2="315" y2="70" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
    <line x1="315" y1="116" x2="315" y2="128" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
  </svg>
  </div>
  <div class="cap">"There are no cold blocks" is true at tier 3, and only at tier 3. Tiers
  1 and 2 are where a real server spends most of its time.</div>
</div>

<p>The mechanism has a right answer — release the victim's blocks, requeue it, treat the
resume as a prefill. The <b>policy</b> has only defensible ones. Mine came from one
question: <em>is anything guaranteed to finish?</em> If a request is one token from done,
preempting it throws away everything it has built for no gain. So protect whoever is
closest to finishing, and preempt whoever has the most left to do:</p>

<pre><code><span class="k">def</span> select_victim(self, requester):
    <span class="c"># Protect whoever is closest to done; preempt whoever has furthest to go.</span>
    <span class="k">return</span> max(self.running, key=<span class="k">lambda</span> r: r.max_new_tokens - r.generated)</code></pre>

<p>The baseline I put in as a placeholder preempts the most recently scheduled request —
roughly what vLLM does, which preempts in reverse order of its running queue. (That's
LIFO by scheduling recency, which stops being the same thing as arrival order once a
queue exists; my harness approximates it with arrival order.) On five requests and a pool
oversubscribed 1.8×, my policy won comfortably:</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>policy</th><th>steps</th><th>preemptions</th><th>tokens recomputed</th><th>worst-hit request</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr class="win"><td>most-remaining (mine)</td><td>16</td><td><span class="delta">3</span></td><td><span class="delta">24</span></td><td>2×</td></tr>
    <tr><td>newest-arrival (vLLM)</td><td>18</td><td>16</td><td>64</td><td>9×</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Then I ran a second workload: steady short traffic with one long request mixed in, 14
requests, oversubscribed 3.5×. It reversed the result.</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>policy · open workload</th><th>steps</th><th>preemptions</th><th>tokens recomputed</th><th>worst-hit request</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr class="lose"><td>most-remaining (mine)</td><td>23</td><td>3</td><td class="bad">25</td><td>3×</td></tr>
    <tr class="win"><td>newest-arrival (vLLM)</td><td><span class="delta">19</span></td><td>4</td><td><span class="delta">5</span></td><td>2×</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Five times the wasted recompute, and four steps slower. The policy that won the first
test loses once the workload includes a request that doesn't look like the others.</p>

<p>There are two structural reasons, and I only understood the first before running
this. Cheapest-to-preempt and fairest-to-preempt pull in opposite directions, through a
feedback loop: the least-progressed request is always the cheapest victim, and preempting
it keeps it the least-progressed, so it stays the cheapest victim. The arm that does this
deliberately — <code class="inl">cheapest-recompute</code>, evict whoever holds the fewest
tokens — posts the best recompute score of all six policies on the closed workload, 13
tokens. It pays for that score with eleven preemptions, five landing on R3 and five on R4,
each costing zero recomputed tokens because neither request had produced anything to throw
away. Two of five requests spent the run being bounced back to the queue, and the
scoreboard called it a win.</p>

<p>The second reason is the one the open workload exposed, and it's about
<em>information</em>:</p>

<p>Look at what each policy measures, and who sets that number. Newest-arrival reads arrival
time — a field the scheduler has no control over, fixed before the request entered the queue,
and unchanged by anything the scheduler decides. Most-remaining reads
<code class="inl">max_new_tokens - generated</code>, and preempting a request is precisely
what stops <code class="inl">generated</code> from growing. The decision feeds back into the
number the decision is made on. That is why my victim never changed and vLLM's did: a
<b>dependent</b> key selects the same request forever, while an <b>independent</b> one lets
the winner rotate as new requests arrive.</p>

<div class="next">
  <b>My key is also cheating.</b> <code class="inl">max_new_tokens - generated</code> is
  remaining <em>budget</em>, not remaining <em>work</em>. Generation stops on an EOS token;
  <code class="inl">max_new_tokens</code> is a ceiling the client asked for, not a
  prediction. This is shortest-job-first in disguise — provably optimal for mean turnaround
  time, and dependent on knowing job length in advance, which schedulers never do.
</div>

<h2><span class="n">06</span>What's next — and what I got wrong</h2>

<p>The state of the build: the allocator, the gather, and the policy all exist and all
run, and the scheduler still recomputes. Wiring <code class="inl">paged_kv</code> into
<code class="inl">scheduler.py</code> and benchmarking against the recompute baseline from
the last post is the next step. Until that lands this is three components, not a working
cache.</p>

<p>Two things I got wrong.</p>

<p>My first comparison harness was broken, and it read as a finding. The initial open
workload asked for a request larger than the entire pool, so every policy starved
identically and all six tied. Six identical rows looked, for about a minute, like a
result: policy doesn't matter. A benchmark where every arm ties is a bug report, not a
conclusion.</p>

<p>And the fixed harness still under-discriminates. Three or four preemptions per run is
a small sample, and <code class="inl">oldest-arrival</code> produces numbers
byte-identical to mine on both workloads, which means these workloads don't separate
those two policies at all. The tables above are directional. Making them a verdict needs
runs long enough for policies to diverge and workloads built so that different victims
actually get chosen.</p>

<p>Not covered here, because they're adjacent to the build and not in it: prefix caching
and copy-on-write, swap-to-CPU as the alternative to recompute-on-resume, and kernel
fusion.</p>

<p>One thing I'd want to know before trusting any of this at scale: my policy assumes
preemption is the interesting decision, but tiers 1 and 2 of that allocator — the free
list and the prefix cache — are where a production server spends nearly all its time.
A better prefix-cache hit rate probably beats any victim-selection policy, and I have no
measurement either way.</p>

<p>Full code: <a href="https://github.com/balachidam21/nanogpt-served">github.com/balachidam21/nanogpt-served</a>
— <code class="inl">paged_kv.py</code>, <code class="inl">paged_attention.py</code>,
<code class="inl">paged_scheduler.py</code>, <code class="inl">compare_policies.py</code>.
Feedback very welcome, particularly on the harness.</p>
